(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["clinical-medicine-and-surgery-i-exam-5-cardiomyopathy-quiz"]={"title":"Cardiomyopathy Quiz 1 — CMS I Exam 5","category":"Clinical Medicine and Surgery I","exam":"Exam 5","sem":"fall-2026","questions":[{"q":"Why is a patient with cardiomyopathy referred to cardiology?","choices":["Only once symptoms occur at rest","Only when a device must be implanted","Only after an admission for edema","To establish care and review risk features"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Referral is to establish care, consider genetic testing, review any high-risk features, and discuss medications or the need for an intervention. — Cardiomyopathy - Carter.pptx, Slide 15"},{"q":"Which group is particularly susceptible to acute myocarditis?","choices":["Smokers","Young males","Older women","Infants"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. There is no race predilection and incidence is similar between the sexes, although young males are particularly susceptible. — Cardiomyopathy - Carter.pptx, Slide 50"},{"q":"How often is heart muscle biopsy useful in dilated cardiomyopathy?","choices":["Rarely; not used routinely","Always, to confirm it","Routinely, before drugs","Yearly, for monitoring"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Biopsy is not used routinely and is rarely useful in dilated cardiomyopathy; it is reserved for rapidly deteriorating function or systemic diseases that may involve the heart. — Cardiomyopathy - Carter.pptx, Slide 41"},{"q":"Which device therapies are part of adult cardiomyopathy care?","choices":["Carotid stent or vena cava filter devices","Temporary external pacing, as needed","Defibrillator or biventricular pacemaker","Intra-aortic balloon pump for all patients"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Adult device therapy is an implantable cardioverter-defibrillator or a biventricular pacemaker, with an assist device or transplant considered in advanced cases. — Cardiomyopathy - Carter.pptx, Slide 105"},{"q":"Which laboratory results are typical of Takotsubo (stress) cardiomyopathy?","choices":["High lipase and amylase","Raised troponin and peptide levels","Normal troponin, normal peptide","High ferritin and transferrin"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Troponin I and T are usually elevated and B-type natriuretic peptide is frequently elevated, which adds to the resemblance to acute coronary syndrome. — Cardiomyopathy - Carter.pptx, Slide 103"},{"q":"When is cardiac catheterization obtained in hypertrophic cardiomyopathy?","choices":["Yearly, for surveillance","Before any beta blocker","Before a pacemaker or surgery","To make the diagnosis"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Catheterization is not required for diagnosis; it is obtained when invasive therapy, a pacemaker or surgery, is planned. — Cardiomyopathy - Carter.pptx, Slide 75"},{"q":"Why may a permanent pacemaker be needed in restrictive cardiomyopathy?","choices":["Outflow obstruction on exertion","A low ejection fraction on echo","Recurrent atrial thrombus","Nodal fibrosis causing heart block"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Fibrosis of the sinoatrial and atrioventricular nodes can lead to heart block, so a permanent pacemaker is placed for conduction system disease. — Cardiomyopathy - Carter.pptx, Slide 99"},{"q":"Which heart sound is often present in hypertrophic cardiomyopathy?","choices":["S4, from a noncompliant ventricle","S3, from a compliant ventricle","An opening snap after S2","An ejection click before S2"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. An S4 heart sound is often present because the left ventricle is noncompliant; blood pressure and heart rate are usually normal and the apical impulse is forceful and sustained. — Cardiomyopathy - Carter.pptx, Slide 67"},{"q":"What is the diastolic abnormality of hypertrophic cardiomyopathy without obstruction?","choices":["Reduced compliance, high filling pressure","Increased compliance, low filling pressure","Weak contraction and a huge cavity","Pericardial fluid limiting filling"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. The hypertrophied myocardium resists diastolic filling (reduced compliance), and the elevated left ventricular end-diastolic pressure reflects that decreased compliance. — Cardiomyopathy - Carter.pptx, Slide 61"},{"q":"Besides valve regurgitation, which complications does dilated cardiomyopathy put patients at risk for?","choices":["Arrhythmias and thromboembolism","Outflow obstruction and syncope","Heart block from nodal fibrosis","Plaque rupture and infarction"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Rising end-systolic volumes drive further dilation and dysfunction, and patients are at risk for arrhythmias and thromboembolic complications. — Cardiomyopathy - Carter.pptx, Slide 19"},{"q":"Which side of heart failure often dominates in restrictive cardiomyopathy?","choices":["Left-sided failure","Neither side at all","High-output failure","Right-sided failure"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Even though filling pressures are elevated in both ventricles, right heart failure is often more prominent: hepatomegaly, bilateral pedal edema and ascites. — Cardiomyopathy - Carter.pptx, Slide 90"},{"q":"Why is a high degree of suspicion needed to identify acute myocarditis?","choices":["It only affects older adults","Biopsy is never diagnostic","Many cases are not clinically obvious","Troponin is always normal in it"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Because many cases of myocarditis are not clinically obvious, a high degree of suspicion is required to identify it. — Cardiomyopathy - Carter.pptx, Slide 56"},{"q":"What proportion of patients with mild myocarditis later develop dilated cardiomyopathy?","choices":["Nearly all","None","One third","One half"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Most patients with mild symptoms recover completely, but one third subsequently develop dilated cardiomyopathy. — Cardiomyopathy - Carter.pptx, Slide 50"},{"q":"Which region does the hypertrophy of hypertrophic cardiomyopathy most frequently involve?","choices":["Right ventricular wall","Left atrial wall","Ventricular apex only","Interventricular septum"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. The hypertrophy is often asymmetric and can involve any region of the left ventricle, but it most frequently involves the interventricular septum. — Cardiomyopathy - Carter.pptx, Slide 58"},{"q":"What are the most common symptoms of Takotsubo (stress) cardiomyopathy?","choices":["Claudication and pallor","Fever and myalgia first","Ascites and pedal edema","Chest pain and dyspnea"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Chest pain and dyspnea are the most common symptoms; palpitations, nausea, vomiting and syncope also occur, and the examination is nonspecific. — Cardiomyopathy - Carter.pptx, Slide 102"},{"q":"Why must diuretics be used cautiously in restrictive cardiomyopathy?","choices":["They worsen iron overload","They thicken the walls","Stiff ventricles need preload","They cause heart block"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Diuretics treat pulmonary and systemic congestion, but they lower preload, and noncompliant ventricles depend on preload to maintain cardiac output. — Cardiomyopathy - Carter.pptx, Slide 98"},{"q":"Which serious outcome can the arrhythmias of acute myocarditis cause?","choices":["Septal hypertrophy","Pericardial effusion","Sudden cardiac death","Aortic dissection"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Palpitations, syncope or sudden cardiac death can result from underlying ventricular arrhythmias or atrioventricular block in acute myocarditis. — Cardiomyopathy - Carter.pptx, Slide 51"},{"q":"Which infection from beta-hemolytic streptococcus involves the heart in 50% to 90% of cases?","choices":["Q fever","Rheumatic fever","Lyme disease","Diphtheria"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Rheumatic fever, from beta-hemolytic streptococcus, affects the heart in 50% to 90% of cases and is among the infectious causes of myocarditis. — Cardiomyopathy - Carter.pptx, Slide 48"},{"q":"Which activity advice is given to patients with hypertrophic cardiomyopathy?","choices":["Competitive sport if asymptomatic","Complete bed rest indefinitely","High-intensity interval training","Mild to moderate, noncompetitive"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Patients have historically been told to confine themselves to mild to moderate recreational activity in a noncompetitive manner, since sudden death relates to extreme exertion. — Cardiomyopathy - Carter.pptx, Slide 82"},{"q":"Which echocardiogram pattern fits restrictive cardiomyopathy?","choices":["Big atria, near-normal ventricles","Dilated, thin-walled ventricles","Small cavity, septal bulge","Apex akinetic, base normal"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. The echocardiogram shows marked enlargement of one or both atria, near-normal left ventricular systolic function and cavity size, preserved ejection fraction and abnormal diastolic function. — Cardiomyopathy - Carter.pptx, Slide 96"},{"q":"In dilated cardiomyopathy, which patients receive anticoagulation?","choices":["Only patients whose cause is viral infection","Atrial fibrillation, prosthetic valve or clot","Every patient from diagnosis, lifelong use","Any patient with a reduced ejection fraction"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Anticoagulants should be restricted to patients in atrial fibrillation, with artificial valves or with a known mural thrombus. — Cardiomyopathy - Carter.pptx, Slide 42"},{"q":"Which drugs may benefit patients with restrictive cardiomyopathy?","choices":["Antibiotics, steroids or antivirals","Beta blockers, verapamil or diltiazem","Digoxin, epinephrine or dopamine","Nifedipine, nitrates or hydralazine"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Beta blockers and the calcium channel blockers verapamil or diltiazem may help by lengthening filling time, improving relaxation and reducing sympathetic stimulation. — Cardiomyopathy - Carter.pptx, Slide 98"},{"q":"What characterizes restrictive cardiomyopathy?","choices":["Transient apical ballooning after stress","Impaired filling, near-normal chamber size","Dilated chambers with thin, weak walls","Asymmetric septal thickening of 1.5 cm"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Restrictive cardiomyopathy is impaired diastolic filling with reasonably preserved left ventricular chamber size and wall thickness normal or slightly increased. — Cardiomyopathy - Carter.pptx, Slide 84"},{"q":"Which medication is a recognized cause of myocarditis?","choices":["Amiodarone","Methyldopa","Metoprolol","Lisinopril"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Medications that cause myocarditis include penicillin, thiazide diuretics, tetracycline and methyldopa; toxins include lithium, arsenic, lead and copper. — Cardiomyopathy - Carter.pptx, Slide 49"},{"q":"Which parasite causes dilated cardiomyopathy through Chagas disease?","choices":["Toxoplasma gondii","Plasmodium falciparum","Giardia lamblia","Trypanosoma cruzi"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Trypanosoma cruzi causes Chagas disease, a parasitic infectious myocarditis that leads to dilated cardiomyopathy; it is spread by triatomine (kissing) bugs. — Cardiomyopathy - Carter.pptx, Slide 22"},{"q":"How should suspected Takotsubo (stress) cardiomyopathy be evaluated and treated at first?","choices":["As acute coronary syndrome","As a panic attack at home","As viral myocarditis first","As restrictive disease"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Because it mimics acute coronary syndrome with no reliable early differences, evaluation and treatment follow established acute coronary syndrome protocols, with admission to cardiology. — Cardiomyopathy - Carter.pptx, Slide 104"},{"q":"What do Kerley B lines on a chest radiograph indicate?","choices":["Pulmonary edema","Pleural effusion","Pneumothorax","Lung fibrosis"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Kerley B lines are 1-2 cm horizontal lines meeting the pleura at right angles, and they indicate pulmonary edema; the film may also show cardiomegaly and effusions. — Cardiomyopathy - Carter.pptx, Slide 39"},{"q":"Which self-care point is advised in hypertrophic cardiomyopathy?","choices":["Stay well hydrated","Limit fluids strictly","Drink more alcohol","Use nitrates freely"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Patients maintain hydration and avoid dehydration, which lowers preload and worsens obstruction, and they avoid excess alcohol. — Cardiomyopathy - Carter.pptx, Slide 82"},{"q":"Which echocardiogram finding confirms a clinical diagnosis of hypertrophic cardiomyopathy?","choices":["Apical ballooning with a normal base","Pericardial thickening on the images","Unexplained wall thickness of 1.5 cm or more","Ejection fraction under 35% with dilation"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. The diagnosis is confirmed by unexplained increased left ventricular wall thickness of 1.5 cm or more anywhere in the wall, most often asymmetric septal hypertrophy. — Cardiomyopathy - Carter.pptx, Slide 72"},{"q":"What does a normal electrocardiogram suggest when dilated cardiomyopathy is suspected?","choices":["It has no value here","It almost rules it out","It confirms early disease","It points to alcohol"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. The electrocardiogram is usually abnormal in dilated cardiomyopathy, so a normal tracing will almost rule the diagnosis out. — Cardiomyopathy - Carter.pptx, Slide 37"}]};
